.TH CONFIGSYS.HU 5 "" "configsys" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
configsys.hu \- the configsys configuration and routing format
.PP
configsys reads Humon (\fB.hu\fR) files. This is the reference for their structure: how your per-machine config selects what to install, how \fBroutes.hu\fR describes where each component comes from on any OS, and how the two combine. It is the source for the \fBconfigsys.hu(5)\fR man page and is linked from the README.
.SH LAYERS
.PP
Every \fB.hu\fR file is a \fBlayer\fR. Layers overlay section-by-section, lowest precedence first, and the highest-precedence definition of a thing wins:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
repo (routes.hu + config.hu)  <  plugins  <  primary  <  discovered project files  <  your config
.fi
.RE
.PP
Your machine's own config (\fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR) always wins. The \fBprimary\fR layer is one plugin you designate as trusted to carry machine settings (see docs/plugins.md) — it sits above the ordinary plugins but below your config. A file may \fBinclude:\fR other files (paths relative to the including file's directory), which sit just below it. Includes are definitions-only: their \fBcomponents:\fR and \fBprofiles:\fR merge in, but machine settings (\fBconfigs:\fR, \fBscope:\fR, \fBpins:\fR, \fBignore-profiles:\fR, \fBdiscover:\fR) and code-adjacent sections (\fBos:\fR, \fBdrivers:\fR) are ignored. The one exception is \fBtheme:\fR, which is purely cosmetic and is merged from every layer (see docs/theming.md).
.SH YOUR CONFIG FILE
.PP
Lives under \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR (defaults to \fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR); a legacy \fB~/configsys.hu\fR is migrated automatically on first run. Every section is optional:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{
    configs: [ dev ]                 // which profiles apply to THIS machine

    scope: system                    // default install scope for scope-honoring drivers

    include: [ ~/src/myproject/configsys.hu ]   // pull in more profiles/components

    plugins: [ { source: "github:someone/configsys-opensuse"  ref: v1.2.0 } ]

    pins: { steam: flatpak }         // force a driver (binding-pin) or a provider

    driver-preference: [ native, flatpak, appImage ]   // tiebreak among valid methods

    profiles: { dev: [ btop, neovim, gcc-15, gdb ] }   // define or shadow a profile

    components: { apod: {} }         // amend a route, add one, or remove one with {}

    ignore-profiles: [ gaming ]      // suppress an auto-activated project profile

    discover: false                  // opt out of project discovery on this machine
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBconfigs:\fR\fR is the set of profiles active on this machine.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBscope:\fR\fR sets the default install scope (\fBuser\fR or \fBsystem\fR) for scope-honoring drivers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBpins:\fR\fR reroutes without redefining: a binding-pin forces a component's driver, a provider-pin forces which component satisfies a capability.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdriver-preference:\fR\fR is a global tiebreak order over drivers, used to pick the default when a component has several valid install methods (see "Choosing among methods" below). It replaces the whole list across layers (repo < primary < user).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBcomponents:\fR\fR \fIamends\fR a route: bindings merge \fBadditively\fR across layers by \fB(via, when)\fR identity — a higher layer adds an install method, overrides a matching binding, retracts one with a \fBdrop:\fR binding, or removes the whole component with \fB{}\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBtheme:\fR\fR restyles the TUI (colors, per-element styles, background gradient). It is the one cosmetic section any layer may set; see docs/theming.md.
.SH PROFILES
.PP
A profile is a named list of components. Its value is a list of \fBterms\fR, applied left-to-right:
.IP \(bu 2
a bare \fBname\fR adds a component;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+name\fR splices in another profile's members (recursively);
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~name\fR removes a component added so far;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+self\fR means "the same profile from the next layer down", so a higher layer \fBamends\fR a profile in place instead of replacing it wholesale.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
profiles: {
    base: [ btop, git, curl ]
    dev:  [ +base, neovim, gcc-15, ~curl ]   // base, plus tools, minus curl
    dev:  [ +self, valgrind ]                // (higher layer) amend dev in place
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Order matters: a \fB~\fR after a \fB+\fR drops what the include brought in; a later add re-adds it.
.PP
\fB\fBall\fR\fR is a built-in synthetic profile — every defined component. You don't declare it; use it as \fBconfigsys install profile:all\fR or add it to \fBconfigs:\fR to browse the full menu in \fBinspect\fR/ the TUI (it shows as a \fB+all\fR note rather than a listed profile, and \fBinspect\fR resolves it resiliently, so components that don't route on this OS just show as errors rather than blocking).
.SH COMPONENTS AND BINDINGS
.PP
\fBroutes.hu\fR's \fBcomponents:\fR section defines each component as a capability plus a list of context-selected \fBbindings\fR. A binding names its \fBdriver\fR with \fBvia:\fR, guards itself with an optional \fBwhen:\fR expression, and carries driver-specific details:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
components: {
    btop: {
        install: [
            { via: native  when: "rhel"  requires: epel }   // EL: needs EPEL enabled
            { via: native  repo-component: universe }        // everywhere else
        ]
    }
    chrome:     { install: [ { via: flatpak  hub: flathub  app: com.google.Chrome } ] }
    vulkan-dev: { install: [ { via: parts  parts: [ build-essential, vulkan-sdk ] } ] }
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBvia: native\fR\fR resolves to whatever the OS uses (apt / dnf / pacman / zypper / apk / brew) — one route covers every distro. \fBname:\fR maps the package name per driver.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBvia: parts\fR\fR is a pure aggregator: a component that is just the union of its parts, with no unit of its own.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBrequires:\fR\fR pulls in capabilities (another component, or a driver's prerequisites) first, dependency-ordered — a hard dependency (unmet is an error). \fB\fBsuggests:\fR\fR is soft (pulled in if resolvable here, skipped otherwise). \fB\fBprovides:\fR\fR declares extra capabilities a component satisfies.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBscope: user|system\fR\fR sets the install scope. Scope-honoring drivers (appImage, flatpak, tarball, source, font, npm, gem, luarocks) default to \fBuser\fR; fixed-scope drivers (apt/dnf/pacman = system, cargo/pipx/dotfiles = user) ignore it.
.PP
Bindings \fBmerge additively\fR across layers. A binding's identity is its \fB(via, when)\fR pair, so a higher layer (a plugin or your config) can \fIadd\fR a new install method, \fIoverride\fR a matching binding, \fIretract\fR an inherited one with a \fBdrop:\fR binding (\fB{ via: X  when: Y  drop }\fR), or clear the whole component with \fB{}\fR — none of which requires redefining the component.
.PP
Among the bindings that are \fBvalid\fR here (their \fBwhen:\fR matches), one is chosen — see "Choosing among methods" below. Run \fBconfigsys where <name>\fR to see a component's bindings, which are valid here, and which one resolves.
.SH THE \FBWHEN:\FR EXPRESSION
.PP
\fBwhen:\fR is a boolean expression that selects a binding by machine context. Its atoms are:
.IP \(bu 2
an \fBOS name\fR — bare (\fBubuntu\fR, \fBredhat\fR) matches that block and everything that inherits from it; versioned (\fBubuntu < 23.04\fR) matches a version range on a scale;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBCPU\fR atom (\fBcpu: aarch64\fR).
.PP
Combine atoms with \fBand\fR, \fBor\fR, and a guarded \fBnot\fR. OS blocks form a lineage via \fBusing:\fR (\fBpop_os! -> ubuntu -> debian -> linux\fR), detected from \fB/etc/os-release\fR (\fBID=pop\fR -> \fBpop_os!\fR), so a route written on \fBdebian\fR applies to the whole family. \fBwhen:\fR expresses \fBvalidity only\fR — whether a method works here — never which method to prefer. Two bindings of the \fBsame driver\fR whose match-sets overlap must be comparable (one more specific than the other) or configsys reports a load-time ambiguity; overlapping bindings of \fIdifferent\fR drivers are legal alternatives, decided by preference (see below).
.SH CHOOSING AMONG METHODS
.PP
A component can have several valid install methods in one context (e.g. \fBnative\fR and \fBflatpak\fR both work on Ubuntu). configsys picks one default deterministically, in this order:
.PP
1. \fBmost specific\fR among comparable valid bindings (a narrower \fBwhen:\fR beats a broader one); 2. \fB\fBdriver-preference\fR\fR — the global tiebreak list (overridable per OS block); 3. a per-binding \fB\fBprefer:\fR\fR rank.
.PP
If that still ties, it is an error that names the preference channel — never a prompt to narrow a validity \fBwhen:\fR. To override the default on one machine, \fBconfigsys pin set <name> <driver>\fR writes a binding-pin (or edit \fBpins:\fR directly); \fBconfigsys where <name>\fR shows all candidates and which rule decided.
.PP
A derivative distro that does not rebrand \fB/etc/os-release\fR (Proxmox VE reports \fBID=debian\fR) can still be detected by a \fBmarker\fR: an os block declares \fBdetect: { id: <base>  marker: <path> }\fR (marker may be a list). When the detected block is that base — or a descendant — and every marker exists on disk, configsys routes to the more-specific block. This is the data-driven form of the built-in Fedora-Atomic ostree-marker detection, so a plugin can add a detectable OS with no code (the \fBconfigsys-proxmox\fR plugin does exactly this).
.SH VERSIONS — DISCOVERED, NOT HARDCODED
.PP
Download-based bindings declare \fIhow\fR to find the latest version rather than pinning one:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
neovim: {
    install: [ { via: appImage  name: Neovim  scope: user
                 version: { github: neovim/neovim  asset: "nvim-linux-$ARCH.appimage" }
                 url: "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases/download/$VERSION/nvim-linux-$ARCH.appimage" } ]
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ github: owner/repo }\fR\fR — the latest release tag; optional \fBstrip-v\fR; optional \fBasset: <glob>\fR also resolves the exact download URL from the release assets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ url: "..."  regex: "..." }\fR\fR — fetch a page and extract the version.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ static: "..." }\fR\fR — a deliberate pin.
.PP
\fB$VERSION\fR and \fB$ARCH\fR are filled into the URL at install time. When a \fBgithub\fR asset name is literal (no glob), configsys can fall back to the API-free \fBreleases/latest/download/<asset>\fR URL, so installs keep working when the GitHub API is unreachable. Discovered versions are cached (\fB~/.config/configsys/versions.hu\fR, 24h TTL); \fBconfigsys refresh\fR re-queries; and \fB--pretend\fR never touches the network (cache-only). Set \fBCONFIGSYS_GITHUB_TOKEN\fR (or \fBGITHUB_TOKEN\fR) to lift GitHub's unauthenticated rate limit.
.SH DOTFILES
.PP
A \fBvia: dotfiles\fR component maps link specs \fB{ src, dst }\fR: \fBdst\fR is where the config belongs (env-var and \fB~\fR expanded), and \fBsrc\fR is resolved through a content \fBsearch-path\fR — the first of these that has it wins:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~/.config/configsys/dotfiles/<src>\fR — your machine-local store
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<primary plugin>/dotfiles/<src>\fR — your portable, git-tracked config
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<defining layer>/dotfiles/<src>\fR — a template, only if some layer ships one
.PP
configsys ships \fBno personal config templates\fR: a component may declare \fBsrc\fR/\fBdst\fR with no content anywhere, in which case it is simply not linked (a no-op) until you supply content. Install symlinks \fBdst -> src\fR so edits flow back to git.
.PP
Because your existing config is precious, install \fBrefuses\fR to symlink over a real on-system file that resolves only to an un-adopted template, printing how to adopt it (\fBconfigsys dotfiles capture\fR) — or \fBinstall --force\fR to back it up to \fB<name>.pre-configsys\fR and replace. Adopted content (in your store) links freely; an \fBabsorb-into\fR spec relocates a pre-existing file into a loader dir instead of backing it up (e.g. a stray \fB~/.bash_aliases\fR). \fBuninstall\fR removes the symlink and restores any backup.
.PP
\fBconfigsys dotfiles status\fR reports each dotfile's state — \fBlinked\fR / \fBadopted\fR / \fBunmanaged\fR / \fBtemplate\fR / \fBempty\fR — and where its managed content lives; \fBconfigsys dotfiles capture\fR copies your existing on-system dotfiles into your store (read-only on the system side). A package that ships config \fBsuggests:\fR its \fB<name>-dotfiles\fR component (soft, so it attaches only where that config exists).
.SH COMPONENT-NAMES
.PP
A higher layer (typically a plugin) can patch the package name a component maps to under a given driver — or drop it where that driver has no package — without redefining the whole component:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
component-names: {
    xbps: { docker-engine: docker  r: R  nmap: {} }   // rename; {} = no package here (drop)
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keyed by driver and overlaid across the layer stack (later wins): a string replaces the resolved package name; \fB{}\fR (or null) means "not available via this driver," so the component isn't offered there. See the routing model (docs/routing-model.md §10a) for the full rules.
.SH DRIVERS
.PP
Each \fBvia:\fR value names a driver: the OS package managers (\fBapt\fR, \fBdnf\fR, \fBpacman\fR, \fBzypper\fR, \fBapk\fR, \fBaur\fR, \fBbrew\fR, \fBrpm-ostree\fR), \fBflatpak\fR, \fBappImage\fR, \fBtarball\fR (also bare-binary and \fB.zip\fR archives), \fBsource\fR (build from a git checkout or source archive with declared \fBbuild:\fR commands), \fBdotfiles\fR, \fBfont\fR, \fBscript\fR (declared install/version/uninstall commands), the language toolchains and their module installers (\fBcargo\fR, \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR, \fBnpm\fR, \fBgem\fR, \fBgo-install\fR, \fBopam\fR, \fBluarocks\fR, \fBcabal\fR, \fBgcc\fR, \fBclang\fR, \fBgcc-toolset\fR), and the post-install primitives \fBservice\fR (systemd) and \fBgroup\fR (usermod). Two \fBvia:\fR values are special: \fBnative\fR (resolves to the OS's package manager) and \fBparts\fR (a pure aggregator).
.SH PROJECT DISCOVERY
.PP
configsys walks up from your working directory for \fB.configsys.hu\fR (a base file) and \fB.configsys-*.hu\fR (named variants), and \fBauto-activates\fR their profiles — so a source tree can declare its own dependency set. Discovery is bounded by \fB$HOME\fR and the filesystem root, disabled by \fBCONFIGSYS_NO_DISCOVER=1\fR (or \fBdiscover: false\fR in your config), and starts from \fBCONFIGSYS_CWD\fR (or the current directory). A malformed discovered file is skipped, never fatal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBconfigsys(1)\fR for the command-line interface; the README for a narrative overview.
